Anonymous GET /app → 302 to /accounts/log-in. 

The grep for title found nothing because the html uses `<title>` with phx? let me look. Not needed.

Now the real browser mount check with headless Chrome Canary + CDP:

Chrome Canary binary: /Applications/Google Chrome Canary.app/Contents/MacOS/Google Chrome Canary

Approach: launch headless with --remote-debugging-port=9223, separate user-data-dir. Then Node script:
- GET http://127.0.0.1:9223/json/version to get browser ws endpoint? Easier: create a new tab via PUT /json/new?<url> (Chrome ≥ M113 requires PUT). Actually simpler: use browser-level WebSocket target from /json/version then Target.createTarget & attach via flat session? More complex. Easiest robust path: fetch `http://127.0.0.1:9223/json/list` after launching chrome with initial URL about:blank; pick page target's webSocketDebuggerUrl and connect.

Steps in script:
1. connect to page ws.
2. Runtime.enable, Page.enable, Network.enable.
3. Page.navigate to http://127.0.0.1:4000/accounts/register; wait for loadEventFired / poll for document.readyState.
4. Runtime.evaluate async fetch register with CSRF from meta; return {status}. Use `awaitPromise` and `returnByValue`. body: URLSearchParams email/password. cookies included same-origin.
5. Page.navigate to /app. Wait a bit; poll via Runtime.evaluate:
   - `document.querySelector('#app') && document.querySelector('#app').getAttribute('data-v-app')`
   - `document.querySelector('#app')?.textContent`
   Wait until text includes "ok".
6. Print result JSON.

CDP over websocket: implement with the global WebSocket. Need message id correlation. Write a helper.

Chrome path might require `--no-sandbox`? On macOS not. Use headless=new flag: "--headless=new". Might need `--disable-gpu`. Provide `--remote-allow-origins=*` for ws.

Alternatively use `--dump-dom` cannot login (no JS POST cookie persistence interplay? Actually dump-dom executes JS then prints DOM; we could do the whole flow in a single page via fetch! Approach: navigate to register page? --dump-dom loads a single URL; JS runs after load. We can navigate to `http://127.0.0.1:4000/accounts/register` and dump DOM — but registering requires POST; --dump-dom can't perform & then navigate to /app in one run. But we can embed an inline data URL script? Hmm: `--dump-dom 'data:text/html,<script>...'` executes script in data URL context, but origin differs (null origin), fetch to 127.0.0.1 cross-origin blocked? data: URLs are opaque origin; CORS would block. Not viable.

CDP route is cleanest. Write /tmp/alur_browser_check.mjs.

Let me write it carefully.

```js
const CDP = async (wsUrl) => {
  const ws = new WebSocket(wsUrl)
  await new Promise((res, rej) => { ws.onopen = res; ws.onerror = rej })
  let id = 0
  const pending = new Map()
  ws.onmessage = (ev) => {
    const msg = JSON.parse(ev.data)
    if (msg.id && pending.has(msg.id)) {
      const { resolve, reject } = pending.get(msg.id)
      pending.delete(msg.id)
      msg.error ? reject(new Error(JSON.stringify(msg.error))) : resolve(msg.result)
    }
  }
  const send = (method, params = {}) => new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const mid = ++id
    pending.set(mid, { resolve, reject })
    ws.send(JSON.stringify({ id: mid, method, params }))
  })
  return { ws, send }
}

const sleep = (ms) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms))

async function main() {
  const base = "http://127.0.0.1:4000"
  const list = await (await fetch("http://127.0.0.1:9223/json/list")).json()
  const page = list.find((t) => t.type === "page")
  if (!page) throw new Error("no page target")
  const cdp = await CDP(page.webSocketDebuggerUrl)
  await cdp.send("Page.enable")
  await cdp.send("Runtime.enable")
  await cdp.send("Network.enable")

  // 1. open the register page (sets the CSRF session cookie)
  await cdp.send("Page.navigate", { url: `${base}/accounts/register` })
  await waitForLoad(cdp)

  // 2. register through a same-origin fetch (session cookies are sent)
  const email = `vuebrowser${Date.now()}@example.com`
  const regResult = await cdp.send("Runtime.evaluate", {
    expression: `(async () => {
      const token = document.querySelector('meta[name="csrf-token"]').content;
      const res = await fetch('/accounts/register', {
        method: 'POST',
        headers: {'content-type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded', 'x-csrf-token': token},
        body: new URLSearchParams({ 'account[email]': ${JSON.stringify(email)}, 'account[password]': 'super secret 1234' })
      });
      return { status: res.status, url: res.url };
    })()`,
    awaitPromise: true,
    returnByValue: true
  })
  console.log("register:", JSON.stringify(regResult.result.value))

  // 3. navigate to /app and wait for Vue mount
  await cdp.send("Page.navigate", { url: `${base}/app` })
  let mounted = null
  for (let i = 0; i < 40; i++) {
    await sleep(250)
    const evalRes = await cdp.send("Runtime.evaluate", {
      expression: `(() => {
        const el = document.querySelector('#app');
        if (!el) return { mounted: false };
        return { mounted: true, dataVApp: el.getAttribute('data-v-app'), health: el.querySelector('[data-health]')?.getAttribute('data-health'), text: el.textContent.trim().slice(0, 120) };
      })()`,
      returnByValue: true
    })
    mounted = evalRes.result.value
    if (mounted?.mounted && (mounted.health === "ok" || mounted.health === "unreachable")) break
  }
  console.log("app state:", JSON.stringify(mounted))
  if (!mounted?.mounted || mounted.health !== "ok") process.exitCode = 1
  cdp.ws.close()
}
```

Wait: two issues — `document.title` check for load; waitForLoad: after Page.navigate, need to wait for load event. Implement waitForLoad via polling document.readyState === 'complete' with Runtime.evaluate.

Also careful: Runtime.evaluate fetch register: does the same-origin fetch to /accounts/register auto-follow the 302 redirect to "/" (pipeline LiveView)? fetch follows redirects by default; the second request GET / includes cookies (account token set on redirect response) and LiveView will connect... Fine. response.url ends at "/".

After that navigate to /app.

LiveView socket maybe connects; irrelevant.

Vue fetch /api/health uses cookies but unauthenticated anyway.

One more consideration: viewport/size; not needed.

Timing: fetch of /app returns HTML quickly; then app.js loads (script defer) executes; then fetch /api/health; ~ immediate. Poll up to 10s.

Chrome launch command (background):
`"/Applications/Google Chrome Canary.app/Contents/MacOS/Google Chrome Canary" --headless=new --disable-gpu --remote-debugging-port=9223 --user-data-dir=/tmp/alur-chrome-profile --no-first-run --no-default-browser-check about:blank`

Chrome Canary may emit noise. Wait for http://127.0.0.1:9223/json/version 200.

Let me implement, being careful about the sandbox writing to /tmp — allowed with danger-full-access (policy: unrestricted).

Note headless chrome on macOS needs `--headless` old vs new; canary supports --headless=new. If it fails, use plain --headless.

Let me write the node script to /tmp/alur_vue_browser_check.mjs and chrome launch.

